/**
 * @mainpage wlonvif C++ API
 *
 * `wlonvif` is a C++20 library for ONVIF Device, Media, PTZ, discovery, events,
 * and deterministic virtual-device testing.
 *
 * @section getting_started Getting started
 *
 * Create a Runtime, transport, and endpoint policy, then consume the cold task
 * returned by DeviceSession::connect(). Every asynchronous network operation takes
 * CallOptions containing an absolute monotonic deadline and cancellation token.
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * auto runtime_result = wlonvif::Runtime::create();
 * if (!runtime_result) {
 *     return 1;
 * }
 *
 * auto runtime = runtime_result.value();
 * auto transport = wlonvif::make_curl_transport(runtime);
 * auto policy = wlonvif::make_same_origin_policy();
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section components Components
 *
 * - DeviceSession exposes Device operations and service-specific clients.
 * - MediaClient retrieves Media1 profiles and media URIs.
 * - PtzClient controls movement, status, Stop, and presets.
 * - DiscoverySession provides bounded discovery observations.
 * - ManagedEventStream provides bounded events and continuity health signals.
 * - VirtualDeviceTransport supplies deterministic test behavior.
 *
 * @section safety Safety model
 *
 * Inputs are bounded by XmlLimits, advertised endpoints require EndpointPolicy
 * authorization, tasks complete once, and cancellation can report uncertain outcomes
 * for operations that may already have reached a device.
 *
 * @section examples Real-device examples
 *
 * Build with `WLONVIF_BUILD_EXAMPLES=ON`. The suite includes robust discovery,
 * read-only Device inspection, concurrent inventory, endpoint-policy evaluation,
 * and a deterministic virtual-device workflow. Shared support supplies deadlines,
 * JSON escaping, URL redaction, and password input that avoids process arguments.
 * See `README.md` and `WLO-EXAMPLES.md` for usage and the implementation roadmap.
 * Network Device sessions also provide Media1 profiles and URIs, normalized PTZ
 * operations, and managed PullPoint Event streams. The media example can copy a
 * bounded snapshot without decoding it; video playback remains delegated to an
 * external RTSP player.
 *
 * The operational examples add aggregate camera health, bounded multi-camera
 * inspection, optional inventory service columns, printable FFmpeg stream-copy
 * commands, and explicitly confirmed preset tours. Discovery cannot guarantee that
 * multicast-disabled devices will answer; RTSP playback/recording needs an external
 * media stack; TLS certificate verification remains enabled; and vendor behavior can
 * differ. Manual results belong in `docs/compatibility-matrix.md` while ordinary CI
 * stays hardware-independent.
 * Example credentials use command-line values first, then `WLONVIF_USERNAME` and
 * `WLONVIF_PASSWORD`. Anonymous access is tried when neither is present; an HTTP or
 * SOAP authentication failure on an interactive terminal triggers a username and
 * no-echo password prompt followed by one retry. Command-line passwords may be
 * exposed by shell history or process inspection, so environment, protected-file,
 * or prompt input is preferable outside disposable tests.
 * Real-device examples also accept `--ip ADDRESS`; shared support performs targeted
 * library discovery to learn the Device service URL and nonstandard port. `--url`
 * remains the mutually exclusive explicit override.
 * The PTZ diagnostic example exposes bounded individual operations rather than an
 * interactive terminal controller. Applications that need natural manual control
 * should use `PtzController`, which owns input leases, shaping, ramps, command
 * pacing, zoom-aware scaling, watchdog behavior, and Stop/close safety.
 */
